<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hungarian Declaration of Independence</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hungarian Declaration of Independence was adopted by the Hungarian parliament at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a> on 14 April 1849, during the revolution against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. It dethroned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a> and made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> governor-president. Russian intervention crushed the independent state within months, and the Hungarian army surrendered at Világos in August 1849.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hungarian-declaration-of-independence.jpg" alt="Hungarian Declaration of Independence" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Madboy74 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coa_Hungary_Country_History_(1849).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>On 7 December 1848 the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> refused to acknowledge the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> monarch, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a>, on the ground that no one could sit on the Hungarian throne without its knowledge and consent, and called the nation to arms. In Hungarian law a king had to be crowned to exercise royal power, and a crowned king could not give up the throne during his lifetime. Franz Joseph's uncle Ferdinand V was still alive, so in Hungarian eyes Franz Joseph was a usurper and a rebel against the ancient constitution.</p><p>Four causes led to the dethronement: the Imperial Constitution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olmütz</a> of 4 March 1849, which threatened to reduce <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> to a province of a centralised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franz-joseph-i">Franz Joseph</a>'s usurpation; the court's repudiation of the April Laws, sanctioned by Ferdinand V on 11 April 1848; and the attempt of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> army to overthrow Hungary's constitutional government, which the Hungarian government and parliament called an attempted coup by military force.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The successes of the Spring Campaign made the step possible. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lajos-kossuth">Lajos Kossuth</a> presented the declaration to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-assembly-of-france">National Assembly</a> in closed session on 13 April 1849, against the opposition of the Peace Party, and it was proclaimed in the Great Calvinist Church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/debrecen">Debrecen</a>. It severed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg-lorraine">House of Habsburg-Lorraine</a> and dissolved the personal union with the other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> realms; it did not speak of secession from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a>, since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a> had never been constitutionally a part of it. It accused the dynasty of treachery, treason, perjury and war against the nation, and declared that the house of Habsburg had forfeited the throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The act gave the revolt a basis in law, and from then until the revolution's collapse Kossuth, as elected regent-president, was head of state. Later, in a banquet speech before the Corporation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, he urged the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> to recognise Hungarian independence, saying that the declaration had been voted unanimously and that every county and municipality had declared its adherence to it.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hungarian Declaration of Independence” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the spring campaign drove back Austrian forces, the Hungarian parliament, meeting in Debrecen, adopted the <b>Declaration of Independence</b> on 14 April 1849, deposing the Habsburg dynasty and electing Lajos Kossuth governor-president.</blockquote><a href="/state/hungarian-state-1849">Hungarian State (1849)</a></li></ul></section>
